{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Observation\ncuddly-callous-antlion (fixed in 844dd26b854) showed CI unit workers stall for seconds: the py3.11 job spent 4.78 s in a test whose work takes 0.3 s, and the same run took 1.751 s in a fixture that only sets environment variables. Three other \"hang-guard\" tests assert a wall-clock ceiling whose margin is smaller than or close to stalls of that size:\n- `tests/unit/test_doctor_mapping.py:315`: `elapsed < 4.0` against a probe that sleeps 5.0 s; a 4 s stall fails it, and the margin over the 5 s failure mode is 1 s.\n- `tests/unit/test_review_bot.py:2311`: `elapsed < 2.0` for a lifespan shutdown; a 2 s stall fails it.\n- `tests/unit/test_gate_admission_09da.py:168`: `elapsed < 5.0` for a refusal that takes microseconds; a 5 s stall fails it.\n\nFreezing the whole test process (SIGSTOP/SIGCONT from a helper, triggered by a scratch pytest plugin when a function inside the timed window is called) past each ceiling fails all 3 tests in 3 of 3 runs (evidence in comments).\n\n## Expected\nEach test proves its property (the hang was abandoned, the shutdown was bounded, the refusal did not queue) by an ordering or state observation that a descheduled test process cannot break, as 844dd26b854 did for test_llm_timeouts.py.\n\n## Plan\nTest-only change; no production code changes. Each wall-clock assert and its `# timing: hang-guard` marker is removed together (the wall-clock-assert lint only fires on an upper-bound assert, so no marker is needed once the assert is gone).\n1. `test_degradation_slow_probe_times_out_to_unavailable`: the fake probe parks on a `threading.Event` (bounded 30 s wait so the thread always exits) instead of `time.sleep(5.0)`, and sets a `probe_finished` flag when it leaves. After `scan_mapping` returns, assert the probe has NOT finished (it is still parked, so doctor returned without waiting for it) and the single `unavailable` finding's reason names the timeout; release the probe in `finally`. Defect check: `thread.join(timeout)` -> `thread.join()` in `_run_bounded` makes the probe finish before return, so the test fails.\n2. `test_lifespan_cancel_await_is_bounded_for_a_task_slow_to_cancel`: the slow reconciler swallows the first cancel, then sleeps a bounded 30 s and records `finished_on_its_own` if not cancelled again; a second cancel is recorded and honored. Drop the outer `wait_for(..., 10)` safety net and the elapsed assert; assert 2 cancels were delivered and the task did not finish on its own, i.e. lifespan's own `shutdown_cancel_seconds` bound cancelled it. Defect check: replacing the bounded `wait_for(gather(...))` in `lifespan` with a bare `await gather(...)` makes the task finish on its own after 1 cancel, so the test fails (after ~30 s, not a hang).\n3. `test_the_refusal_returns_immediately_rather_than_waiting_for_a_holder`: replace `ga.fcntl` with a proxy whose `flock` records each call's flags and delegates. Assert the refused admission made exactly `limit` (1) lock attempts, each with `LOCK_NB`, i.e. it never asked to wait and never polled. A spy call without `LOCK_NB` fails the test immediately rather than deadlocking. Defect checks: dropping `LOCK_NB` in `_open_slot`, and a retry loop around `_acquire_slot`, each make the test fail.\n4. Re-run each test 3 times under the same freeze; run the 3 test files, `make lint` (including the wall-clock-assert gate), `make typecheck`.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] Each of the 3 tests passes 3 of 3 runs under a pause longer than its former ceiling.\n- [ ] Re-introducing each test's defect makes that test fail.\n- [ ] make lint and make typecheck exit 0, and the change merges with LLM-Review +1 and Verified +1.\n\n## File impact (expected)\n- tests/unit/test_doctor_mapping.py\n- tests/unit/test_review_bot.py\n- tests/unit/test_gate_admission_09da.py"}},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1793488123625663001,"uuid":"98e27754-aa31-43ce-afeb-ef6b9ba6a551"}